{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SgYJBd8oSBUkgFX6RMEAJb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Token-based hash table.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! Replaces the C `picohash_table` (an open-addressed table with\n4\t//! chained collision lists, intrusive `picohash_item`s embedded in\n5\t//! the caller's structs and a function-pointer trio for hashing /\n6\t//! comparing / projecting the embedded item).\n7\t//!\n8\t//! ## API shape\n9\t//!\n10\t//! `HashTable<K, V>` is a slotmap-backed hash map.  `K: Hash + Eq`\n11\t//! provides the key contract; `V` is whatever the caller stores\n12\t//! (usually a token into some other arena — see Phase-4 plan\n13\t//! below).  The trio of C function pointers is gone: hashing comes\n14\t//! from `K`'s `Hash` impl, comparison from `Eq`, and the table\n15\t//! never reaches into the caller's struct so no `key_to_item`\n16\t//! callback is needed.\n17\t//!\n18\t//! Operations return [`HashToken`]s — opaque (`idx, generation`)\n19\t//! handles into this table's slot vector.  Tokens are `Copy`,\n20\t//! survive insertion and removal of *other* keys, but become stale\n21\t//! the moment the keyed entry is removed (the slot's generation\n22\t//! bumps).  [`HashTable::get`] returns `None` for stale tokens —\n23\t//! the safe equivalent of the C dangling-`HashItem*` use-after-free.\n24\t//!\n25\t//! ## Phase 4 plan — implementation\n26\t//!\n27\t//! The bodies in this module are `todo!()`.  Phase 4 picks one of:\n28\t//!\n29\t//! 1. **Hand-roll the slotmap.**  Internal layout (illustrative):\n30\t//!\n31\t//!    ```ignore\n32\t//!    struct Slot<K, V> {\n33\t//!        generation: u32,\n34\t//!        state: SlotState<K, V>,\n35\t//!    }\n36\t//!    enum SlotState<K, V> {\n37\t//!        Free   { next_free: Option<u32> },\n38\t//!        Filled { hash: u64, key: K, value: V, next_in_bin: Option<u32> },\n39\t//!    }\n40\t//!    pub struct HashTable<K, V> {\n41\t//!        slots: Vec<Slot<K, V>>,\n42\t//!        free:  Option<u32>,\n43\t//!        bins:  Vec<Option<u32>>,\n44\t//!        seed:  [u8; 16],\n45\t//!        len:   usize,\n46\t//!    }\n47\t//!    ```\n48\t//!\n49\t//!    `next_in_bin` lives inside the slot, not the user struct, so\n50\t//!    no `unsafe`.  Lookup hashes `K` to a bin index, walks the\n51\t//!    chain by following `next_in_bin` slot indices, comparing each\n52\t//!    `slot.key` against the target.  Insert pushes a new slot\n53\t//!    (or pops one off the free list) and threads it onto the bin\n54\t//!    chain.  Remove unlinks, marks the slot `Free`, bumps\n55\t//!    generation, and adds it to the free list.\n56\t//!\n57\t//! 2. **Build on the `slotmap` crate.**  `slotmap::SlotMap` already\n58\t//!    provides the slot/generation/free-list machinery; this module\n59\t//!    layers the bin-array indexing on top.  The crate is\n60\t//!    `no_std`-friendly with the right features and would let us\n61\t//!    delete maybe 80% of the implementation.\n62\t//!\n63\t//! Either implementation should keep `HashToken` byte-compatible\n64\t//! with the public API below — the choice is local.\n65\t//!\n66\t//! ## Phase 4 plan — call sites\n67\t//!\n68\t//! In the C tree, every hash table is intrusive: the value type\n69\t//! embeds a `HashItem` field and the table's bins point straight\n70\t//! into the middle of those user-owned objects.  In the Rust port\n71\t//! the embedding turns into a *membership token*: the parent\n72\t//! struct holds `Option<HashToken>` for each table it can be in,\n73\t//! and the table's slot stores the parent's `ConnectionToken`\n74\t//! (or other token type) into the arena that owns the parent.\n75\t//!\n76\t//! For example, `quic.connection_by_id: HashTable<ConnectionId,\n77\t//! ConnectionToken>` points at connections that live in\n78\t//! `quic.connections: Arena<Connection>`.  A `Connection` carries\n79\t//! `connection_by_id_membership: Option<HashToken>`; on removal the\n80\t//! Connection hands that token back for an O(1) `connection_by_id.remove`.\n81\t\n82\tuse core::hash::Hash;\n83\tuse core::marker::PhantomData;\n84\t\n85\tuse crate::Error;\n86\t\n87\t/// Opaque handle into a [`HashTable`]'s slot vector.\n88\t///\n89\t/// `idx` selects a slot; `generation` is incremented on every\n90\t/// removal so an old token comparing against a recycled slot\n91\t/// returns `None` from [`HashTable::get`].  The pair is `Copy` and\n92\t/// 64-bit total — call sites pass it by value.\n93\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, Eq, PartialEq, Hash)]\n94\tpub struct HashToken {\n95\t    idx: u32,\n96\t    generation: u32,\n97\t}\n98\t\n99\t/// Hash table mapping `K` to `V`, addressable by [`HashToken`].\n100\t///\n101\t/// The value `V` is typically a token into some other arena (see\n102\t/// the Phase-4 call-site plan in the module docs) but any owned\n103\t/// value works.  `K: Hash + Eq` is the only contract on the key.\n104\tpub struct HashTable<K, V> {\n105\t    /// Phase 4 fills the body — see module docs for the slot /\n106\t    /// bin / free-list shape.\n107\t    _slots: PhantomData<(K, V)>,\n108\t}\n109\t\n110\timpl<K: Hash + Eq, V> HashTable<K, V> {\n111\t    /// Build an empty table with `nb_bin` collision bins, using a\n112\t    /// zero hash seed.\n113\t    ///\n114\t    /// `nb_bin` is fixed at construction (the C source never\n115\t    /// resized any of its tables); pick a value comfortably larger\n116\t    /// than the expected entry count.\n117\t    ///\n118\t    /// C: `picohash_create` (with the per-table `key_to_item`\n119\t    /// callback dropped — Rust never reaches into the user struct).\n120\t    pub fn new(_nb_bin: usize) -> Result<Self, Error> {\n121\t        todo!()\n122\t    }\n123\t\n124\t    /// Like [`HashTable::new`] but with a caller-supplied 16-byte\n125\t    /// hash seed.  picoquic uses one shared seed across every\n126\t    /// per-`Quic` table (see `Quic::hash_seed`) so independent\n127\t    /// `Quic` instances don't share collision patterns.\n128\t    ///\n129\t    /// C: `picohash_create_ex`.\n130\t    pub fn with_seed(_nb_bin: usize, _hash_seed: &[u8; 16]) -> Result<Self, Error> {\n131\t        todo!()\n132\t    }\n133\t\n134\t    /// Insert `(key, value)` and return a [`HashToken`] that can be\n135\t    /// used to retrieve or remove the entry without re-hashing.\n136\t    /// If `key` was already present, the previous value is replaced\n137\t    /// and returned in the `Ok` payload.\n138\t    ///\n139\t    /// Returns [`Error::Memory`] on slot-vector allocation failure.\n140\t    pub fn insert(&mut self, _key: K, _value: V) -> Result<(HashToken, Option<V>), Error> {\n141\t        todo!()\n142\t    }\n143\t\n144\t    /// Look up `key`, returning the matching token or `None`.\n145\t    /// Equivalent to `picohash_retrieve` but typed.\n146\t    pub fn lookup(&self, _key: &K) -> Option<HashToken> {\n147\t        todo!()\n148\t    }\n149\t\n150\t    /// Borrow the value at `token`, or `None` if the token is stale\n151\t    /// (the slot has been recycled) or out of bounds.\n152\t    pub fn get(&self, _token: HashToken) -> Option<&V> {\n153\t        todo!()\n154\t    }\n155\t\n156\t    /// Mutably borrow the value at `token`, or `None` if the token\n157\t    /// is stale.\n158\t    pub fn get_mut(&mut self, _token: HashToken) -> Option<&mut V> {\n159\t        todo!()\n160\t    }\n161\t\n162\t    /// Borrow the `(key, value)` pair at `token`.  Useful when the\n163\t    /// value is a token into another arena and the caller wants\n164\t    /// the original key for re-derivation.\n165\t    pub fn get_key_value(&self, _token: HashToken) -> Option<(&K, &V)> {\n166\t        todo!()\n167\t    }\n168\t\n169\t    /// Remove and return the entry at `token`.  Bumps the slot's\n170\t    /// generation; subsequent [`HashTable::get`] calls with the\n171\t    /// same token return `None`.  Returns `None` if the token was\n172\t    /// already stale.\n173\t    ///\n174\t    /// O(1) — no key re-hashing.  This is the path Phase-4 call\n